    {
      "id": 15,
      "type": "MarkdownNote",
      "pos": [
        0,
        -1500
      ],
      "size": [
        1040,
        620
      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 2,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "## A language model, from nodes\n\nThe same shape as GPT-2, three orders of magnitude smaller and reading letters instead of\nword pieces. Everything a real one does is here; nothing else is.\n\n**The training objective is a shift.** `Text Dataset` cuts the text into overlapping\nwindows and pairs each with itself moved one character to the left. At every position, the\nanswer is the next character. That is the whole of it, and nothing was labelled — the text\nis its own answer.\n\n**Every position is scored.** An ordinary classifier makes one guess per example; this makes\n96 of them, one per character in the window. So the output is `[B, 96, 87]` — batch, time,\none score per character in the vocabulary — and the loss is the mean over every position of\nevery window.\n\nTrains in **about a minute** on a processor with no GPU, on 55,000 characters: the prose of\nthis pack's own README and notes. It learns to write, badly, about node graphs."
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
    {
      "id": 16,
      "type": "MarkdownNote",
      "pos": [
        1080,
        -1500
      ],
      "size": [
        640,
        620
      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 3,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "### Causal is the whole trick\n\nTurn `causal` off in the Transformer Block and the model scores **98% and cannot write a\nword.** It emits a wall of newlines.\n\nAttention lets every position look at every other position. When the answer at position 5\nis the character at position 6, and position 5 can *see* position 6, there is nothing to\nlearn — it copies. Validation loss drops from 2.50 to 0.07, accuracy goes from 29% to 98%,\nand every one of those numbers is worthless, because at writing time there is no future to\ncopy from.\n\n`causal` masks out everything ahead of each position. One toggle, and it is the difference\nbetween a model that has memorised the exercise and one that can finish your sentence.\n\nWorth doing once. A score that good should always be suspicious."
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
    {
      "id": 17,
      "type": "MarkdownNote",
      "pos": [
        1760,
        -1500
      ],
      "size": [
        740,
        620
      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 4,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "### What each piece does\n\n**Embedding** gives every one of the 87 characters its own 128-number vector, learned. That\nis the entire input representation — no features, no rules about vowels.\n\n**Learned Positions** adds a second vector that says *where* in the window a character is.\nAttention is blind to order without it: the same characters shuffled would look identical.\n\n**Transformer Block x3** is the stack. Each block is attention followed by a feed-forward,\nboth wrapped in residual connections and layer norms. `repeat` is depth: set it to 6 and\nyou have a bigger model with no more wiring.\n\n**Linear to 87** turns each position's vector into one score per possible next character.\nThis is where the time axis has to survive — pool it away and there is nothing to predict."
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
    {
      "id": 18,
      "type": "MarkdownNote",
      "pos": [
        2560,
        -1500
      ],
      "size": [
        900,
        380
      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 5,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "### Writing it back out\n\n**Generate (Text)** runs the loop every language model runs: ask for the distribution over\nthe next character, draw one, stick it on the end, ask again.\n\n`temperature` is how boldly to draw. Near 0 it always takes the most likely character and\nfalls into a loop; above about 1.2 it comes apart. 0.7 is a reasonable place to look.\n\nThe `dataset` input is not for data — it carries the vocabulary, which is the only way to\nturn the model's numbers back into letters."
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    }
